; nyrqis.def -- the export table of the boot's SECOND provider, stated as
; ordinals rather than left to the linker's default.
;
; GNU ld's PE backend numbers exports from 1 by default, which would make
; "ordinal 1" and "EAT index 0" the same number -- and a `GetProcAddress`
; that treated the Windows ordinal form as a bare index would then answer
; every question correctly. The ordinal form exists because the two are NOT
; the same: Windows computes `ordinal - OrdinalBase`, and the provider
; declares the base. So this provider declares 10, and `NyrqisBuild` and
; `NyrqisMode` are ordinals 10 and 11 (EAT slots 0 and 1). The boot asks for
; both, requires exactly the addresses their NAMES answer, and requires
; 9 (below the base) and 12 (past the last slot) to resolve to nothing --
; which is a question a base-1 fixture could not put to the implementation.
;
; ld reads this file as an input beside the object; the ordinals are also the
; pin `build.sh` checks out of the linked DLL (`Export Address Table --
; Ordinal Base 10`), so a linker that ignored the base would fail the build.
LIBRARY nyrqis.dll
EXPORTS
NyrqisBuild @10
NyrqisMode @11
